speechtext:
Referring to the red ensign which is hoisted daily on the tower of this building and the arms or ensigns armorial tierced in fesse, what is the height and length in inches and the heraldic signification of
1.	Gules three lions passant guardant in pale or;
2.	a lion rampant within a double tressure flory-counter-flory gules;
3.	azure a harp or stringed argent;
4.	azure three fleurs-de-lis, or;
5.	argent three maple leaves conjoined on one stem proper;
6.	a royal helmet mantled argent doubled gules the crest, that is to say, on a wreath of the colours argent, and
7.	gules a lion passant guardant or imperially crowned proper and holding in the dexter paw
8.	a maple leaf gules;
9.	and supporters on the dexter (a) a lion rampant or holding a lance argent, point or, flying therefrom to the dexter (b) the union flag, and (c) on the sinister a unicorn argent armed crined and unguled or, gorged with (d) a coronet composed of crosses-patee and (e) fleurs-de-lis a chain affixed thereto reflexed of the last, and holding (f) a like lance flying therefrom to the sinister (g) a banner

azure charged with three fleurs-de-lis or; the whole ensigned with (h) the imperial crown proper and below the shield upon a wreath composed of (i) roses, (j) thistles, (k) shamrocks and (1) lilies a scroll azure inscribed with the motto-"A mari usque ad mare"?
10.	Referring to No. 1, what is the official translation of "gules three lions passant guardant in pale or"?
11.	Referring to No. 9, why does the union flag fly to the dexter and why does the banner azure charged with three fleurs-de-lis or fly to the sinister?
12.	Is there any difference between (a) a lion rampant, (b) a lion passant and, (c) a lion passant guardant, and, if so, what is it?
13.	At what date were the armorial bearings of the Dominion of Canada assigned by royal proclamation?
14.	Who were the cabinet ministers of Canada at that time and what departments were they heading?
